## Part 1: Reconstruct

This report tests claims in Alfred Lubrano's 2018 [article](https://whyy.org/articles/new-census-figures-on-philly-neighborhoods-show-inequality-high-numbers-of-whites-living-in-poverty/), including a city poverty rate near 26% in 2013–2017 and large increases in several neighborhoods. Because the article does not publish a tract-to-neighborhood crosswalk, I use census tracts and do not attempt the optional neighborhood aggregation.

I use ACS table B17001: `B17001_002` (population below poverty) divided by `B17001_001` (population for whom poverty status is determined). The 2010 and 2017 ACS five-year releases match the article's 2006–2010 and 2013–2017 periods and provide tract estimates. Because the periods do not overlap, I treat them as independent, although each estimate still summarizes five years.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
poverty_2010 <- get_acs(
  geography = "tract",
  variables = c(
    total = "B17001_001",
    below_poverty = "B17001_002"
  ),
  state = "PA",
  county = "Philadelphia",
  year = 2010,
  survey = "acs5",
  output = "wide"
)

poverty_2017 <- get_acs(
  geography = "tract",
  variables = c(
    total = "B17001_001",
    below_poverty = "B17001_002"
  ),
  state = "PA",
  county = "Philadelphia",
  year = 2017,
  survey = "acs5",
  output = "wide"
)
```
:::


## Part 2: Test

### Poverty rates and margins of error

Because the poverty rate is a proportion, I calculate its 90% margin of error with `moe_prop()`.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
poverty_2010 <- poverty_2010 %>%
  mutate(
    poverty_rate = below_povertyE / totalE,
    poverty_rate_moe = moe_prop(
      num = below_povertyE,
      denom = totalE,
      moe_num = below_povertyM,
      moe_denom = totalM
    )
  )

poverty_2017 <- poverty_2017 %>%
  mutate(
    poverty_rate = below_povertyE / totalE,
    poverty_rate_moe = moe_prop(
      num = below_povertyE,
      denom = totalE,
      moe_num = below_povertyM,
      moe_denom = totalM
    )
  )
```
:::


### Missing values

First I check 2006–2010 for zero denominators or missing estimates.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
missing_2010 <- poverty_2010 %>%
  filter(
    totalE == 0 |
      is.na(poverty_rate) |
      is.na(poverty_rate_moe)
  )

dim(missing_2010)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}

```
[1] 7 8
```


:::
:::


`dim()` reports rows, then columns. The seven rows are tracts with no valid 2006–2010 rate.

The same check for 2013–2017 gives:


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
missing_2017 <- poverty_2017 %>%
  filter(
    totalE == 0 |
      is.na(poverty_rate) |
      is.na(poverty_rate_moe)
  )

dim(missing_2017)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}

```
[1] 8 8
```


:::
:::


Eight tracts have no valid 2013–2017 rate. In both periods, invalid rates come from zero denominators, so I exclude them rather than treat them as zero. Matching leaves 376 usable tracts.

### Reliability

I calculate CV as `(MOE / 1.645) / poverty rate * 100`. Following Jurjevich et al. (2018), CV below 12% is High confidence, 12–40% is Moderate, and above 40% is Low confidence.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
poverty_2010_valid <- poverty_2010 %>%
  filter(
    totalE > 0,
    !is.na(poverty_rate),
    !is.na(poverty_rate_moe)
  ) %>%
  mutate(
    period = "2006–2010",
    poverty_rate_se = poverty_rate_moe / 1.645,
    cv = poverty_rate_se / poverty_rate * 100,
    reliability = case_when(
      cv < 12  ~ "High confidence",
      cv <= 40 ~ "Moderate",
      TRUE     ~ "Low confidence"
    )
  )

poverty_2017_valid <- poverty_2017 %>%
  filter(
    totalE > 0,
    !is.na(poverty_rate),
    !is.na(poverty_rate_moe)
  ) %>%
  mutate(
    period = "2013–2017",
    poverty_rate_se = poverty_rate_moe / 1.645,
    cv = poverty_rate_se / poverty_rate * 100,
    reliability = case_when(
      cv < 12  ~ "High confidence",
      cv <= 40 ~ "Moderate",
      TRUE     ~ "Low confidence"
    )
  )

city_rate_2017 <- sum(poverty_2017_valid$below_povertyE) /
  sum(poverty_2017_valid$totalE)
```
:::


#### Reliability in 2006–2010


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
poverty_2010_valid %>%
  group_by(reliability) %>%
  summarize(
    n_tracts = n()
  ) %>%
  mutate(
    symbol = case_when(
      reliability == "High confidence" ~ "✓",
      reliability == "Moderate" ~ "△",
      TRUE ~ "!"
    ),
    order = case_when(
      reliability == "High confidence" ~ 1,
      reliability == "Moderate" ~ 2,
      TRUE ~ 3
    )
  ) %>%
  arrange(order) %>%
  select(symbol, reliability, n_tracts) %>%
  gt() %>%
  cols_label(
    symbol = "",
    reliability = "Confidence level",
    n_tracts = "Number of tracts"
  ) %>%
  tab_header(
    title = "Reliability of tract poverty-rate estimates",
    subtitle = "Philadelphia, 2006–2010 ACS five-year estimates"
  ) %>%
  tab_source_note(
    "Source: U.S. Census Bureau ACS via tidycensus. Symbols: ✓ High confidence, △ Moderate, ! Low confidence. Jurjevich et al. (2018) thresholds: CV < 12%, 12–40%, and > 40%."
  )
```

